# E13 Game of Life: sequential, fixed order, no random draw, so the C# guarantees the order of every line (exact
# trace, level (a)). 60 turns, 30 generations (the oracle bounds noTurns, tools/csharp-ref), delay 0: the perception is
# O(n²) as in the C# (626 agents), and 120 turns took 240 s under TSan, too close to the time limit. Each "evolve" or
# "update" line of the grid starts a section, followed by the 25 rows of the grid it shows: the rows are compared with
# their section.
cover all
section ^(evolve|update)$
fact phase ^(evolve|update)$
fact row ^([.x]{25})$
same phase seq
same row seq
# the grid acts once per turn after the setup turn, and shows 25 rows each time
check phase count == 59
check row count == 1475
# no exception caught by an agent (the form of the C# drawn before its thread has created it): neither output may
# hold one
fact error (Object reference|out of range|Exception)
optional error
check error count == 0
